## Assignment Is a Move

In XIOM, the `=` operator MOVES ownership, it does NOT copy:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let name = "Alice";
  let greeting = name;  // name MOVED to greeting
  // io.println(name);  // COMPILE ERROR: name has been moved
  io.println(greeting); // Prints "Alice"
  return 0;
}
```

### Moves Are Shallow and Fast

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let v = Vec[Int].new();
  v.push(1);
  v.push(2);
  let v2 = v;   // Just copies the pointer/length/capacity — O(1)!
  // v is now invalid — prevents double-free
  io.println(v2.len());
  return v2.len() as Int;
}
```

### The Compiler Enforces Moves

If you try to use a moved variable, the compiler produces a clear error. This eliminates the dreaded "use-after-free" bug forever.

```xiom
// This WILL NOT COMPILE:
let a = 10;
let b = a;
io.println(a);  // error: use of moved value 'a'
```

### Moves Prevent Double-Free

Without move semantics, both `a` and `b` above might try to free the same memory. XIOM guarantees only one variable owns the value, so it's freed exactly once.

